{
  "dom_id": "step-q104959",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/94f14d4a1876588cbfb2abc4a1542d5961587745a6e24aeae270d15f75f834db.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=7$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is **onto**",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(1)=7$ (we have an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $7\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=7.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=5.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $5\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
